Speech-to-Text can produce both literal and normalized text. 
Literal transcription represents the spoken words closely, 
while normalized text converts certain expressions into a more standard written form, such as changing “twenty five” into “25.”

The service also supports profanity filtering. 
When profanity is detected, you can choose how it appears in the output. 
Remove replaces the profanity with asterisks, 
Mask also hides the word but keeps its first letter, 
and Tag keeps the original word while adding a tag to indicate that it is profanity.

Key idea:
Normalization changes the representation of the text; profanity filtering controls how inappropriate words appear in the output.

